"# aos-directive: resolve directives\n\n```sh\necho '{\"p\": {\"$env\": \"HOME\", \"$path\": \"/x\"}, \"r\": {\"$ref\": \"other.json#/x\"}}' | aos-directive\n# {\"p\": \"/home/me/x\", \"r\": {\"y\": \"hi\"}}\n```\n\nIt reads one JSON document from stdin (empty = `{}`), resolves every directive in it (`$ref`, `$env`, `$path`, ...; relative paths from the current directory; `./` and `../` work) and prints the result as one line. `{\"$ref\": \"#/a/b\"}` (no file name) means `/a/b` of the file that holds the `$ref`: inside a `$ref`'d file that file, at the top the file given by `--file FILE` (also the start of relative paths); without `--file` it is left as it is. After `#`, `..` steps out one level, and stepping out of the root object lands in the folder of the file: `{\"$ref\": \"#/../other.json\"}` is the file next to this one, `{\"$ref\": \"#/../other.json#/k\"}` is `/k` inside it, `#/a/../b` is `#/b`. Add `\"$at\": \"a/b\"` to walk on from where `$ref` points: `{\"$ref\": \"x.json\", \"$at\": \"a/b\"}` is `/a/b` of x.json, `{\"$ref\": \"#/defs\", \"$at\": \"argv\"}` is `/defs/argv` of this file; `$at` takes no `#` (with one the object is left as it is). An unset variable or an unreadable `$ref` = `DirectiveBad`; stdin that is not JSON = `InputBad`. Other aos programs (aos-exec, the clock register, tool registries) resolve the same directives themselves; use this to see what a config will turn into.\n"
